PHASE 5 — DATABRICKS + PYSPARK TRANSFORMATIONS
______________________________________________

This is where most engineering work happened.

Step 1 — Read Bronze Data
_________________________

PySpark Code
df = spark.read.format("parquet").load("/bronze/sales/")

Step 2 — Data Validation
________________________

Checked:
•	Nulls 
•	Duplicates 
•	Invalid values 
•	Data type mismatches 

Example Problem
_______________
transaction_id	amount
1001	-500
Negative sales invalid.

Cleaning Logic
______________
Remove Null Product IDs
df = df.dropna(subset=["product_id"])
Remove Duplicates
df = df.dropDuplicates(["transaction_id"])

Remove Invalid Amounts
______________________
df = df.filter(df.amount > 0)
Step 3 — Standardization
Convert Date Format
from pyspark.sql.functions import to_date
df = df.withColumn(
    "sale_date",
    to_date("sale_date", "yyyy-MM-dd")
)

Step 4 — Business Transformations
__________________________________
Calculate Revenue
from pyspark.sql.functions import col
df = df.withColumn(
    "revenue",
    col("qty") * col("unit_price")
)

Step 5 — Join Multiple Sources
______________________________

•	Sales data 
•	Product master 
•	Customer table 

Join Query
__________

final_df = sales_df.join(
    product_df,
    "product_id",
    "left"
)

Why PySpark?
____________

20+ million records/day
Single-machine Python would become slow.
PySpark distributes processing across clusters.

PERFORMANCE OPTIMIZATION WORK
_____________________________

This is how throughput improved 3x.

Optimization Techniques Used
_____________________________
Technique	-> Purpose
Partitioning	-> Faster reads
Caching	-> Reuse data
Broadcast joins	-> Faster joins
Delta tables	-> Optimized storage

Example Partitioning
____________________

df.write.partitionBy("sale_date")
Example Broadcast Join
from pyspark.sql.functions import broadcast
final_df = sales_df.join(
    broadcast(product_df),
    "product_id"
)

